                <h2>Pre-Generation Checklist</h2>
<p>Before calling AI, complete these preparations:</p>
<p><strong>1. Define specific user scenarios</strong><br />
- ❌ Vague: "Targeting young women"<br />
- ✅ Specific: "Urban women 25-34 in the US searching for affordable skincare on Amazon"</p>
<p><strong>2. Lock in core conversion motivations</strong><br />
- Price-sensitive or quality-oriented?<br />
- What decision stage is the user in?</p>
<p><strong>3. Map out competitor expressions</strong><br />
- List 3-5 main competitors' copy keywords<br />
- Confirm which words you must avoid this round</p>
<h2>Prompt Structure Optimization</h2>
<p><strong>Basic elements (required):</strong><br />
- Specific product/service name<br />
- Detailed target audience profile<br />
- Key USP<br />
- Desired call-to-action</p>
<p><strong>Differentiation instructions (key to standing out):</strong><br />
- "Avoid: best, premium, great value and other empty words"<br />
- "Replace abstract descriptions with specific numbers or scenarios"<br />
- "Incorporate the [specific emotion] that [specific cultural background] users would feel"</p>
<h2>5-Point Post-Generation Filter</h2>
<p><strong>Q1: Could this copy belong to a competitor?</strong><br />
If yes, it's generic.</p>
<p><strong>Q2: What words would real users use to describe this need?</strong><br />
The bigger the gap between AI language and user language, the bigger the problem.</p>
<p><strong>Q3: Can any descriptions be quantified or made specific?</strong><br />
"Dramatically improved" → "Visible changes in 3 weeks"</p>
<p><strong>Q4: Does the emotional layer go beyond one dimension?</strong><br />
Pure "want" or "FOMO" is too thin. Does it layer pride, belonging, or curiosity?</p>
<p><strong>Q5: Does it embed unique brand DNA?</strong><br />
Brand voice, credentials, or distinctive style—present?</p>
<h2>Balancing Efficiency and Differentiation</h2>
<p><strong>Batch Generate + Human Refine Model:</strong><br />
1. Generate 10-15 different-angle drafts with AI<br />
2. Filter 3-5 differentiated versions using the checklist<br />
3. Add brand tone and specific data manually<br />
4. Keep 2-3 differentiated versions for A/B testing</p>
<p><strong>Case Study:</strong> A utility app team targeting Southeast Asia noticed declining CTR on AI-generated "boost efficiency" copy. By adding specific scene descriptions of users on subways and local expressions about time costs to their prompts, then manually replacing overused competitor terms, they achieved a 37% CTR improvement.</p>
<h2>This Week's Action Items</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Compile competitor copy high-frequency word list</li>
<li>[ ] Rewrite AI prompt templates with differentiation instructions</li>
<li>[ ] Run Q1-Q5 checks on current live copy</li>
<li>[ ] Establish "AI draft + human differentiation" standard workflow</li>
<li>[ ] Document effective differentiation elements from each test</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Core Principle:</strong> AI is an accelerator, not a destination. Avoiding generic output isn't about "not using AI"—it's about whether the human operator provided precise scenarios, emotions, and differentiation instructions.</p>
